WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:05.450
Just like with the first one, there are plenty
of things to love about the Zombieland sequel

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:09.690
- but there are also a few things that didn't
work out as well as we might have hoped.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:14.440
From obscene, thrilling action to obnoxious
new characters, let's sink our teeth into

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:18.820
the five best and five worst things about
Zombieland: Double Tap.

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:19.820
Best: Madison

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:23.930
While it's great to see the old crew again,
especially after that weird Amazon series,

00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:28.869
the MVP in Double Tap is quite possibly newcomer
Zoey Deutch's Madison.

00:00:28.869 --> 00:00:32.780
She tags along on the group's quest to find
the missing Little Rock, and the fizzy energy

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:37.170
she brings to the group makes every scene
she's in sparkle with light.

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.590
"Tiny, big, tiny!"

00:00:39.590 --> 00:00:42.010
"She is adorable."

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:46.100
About halfway through the film, it appears
that Madison tragically dies.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:51.050
But in a delightful turn of events, she reappears
out of nowhere in the final act - driving,

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:55.670
appropriately, a technicolor ice cream truck
- giddy to report that what we all assumed

00:00:55.670 --> 00:01:00.390
was a zombie virus infection was actually
just a bad nut allergy.

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:05.329
It's a twist that could feel forced and unearned,
but with Madison, somehow, it works.

00:01:05.329 --> 00:01:06.590
"You're cute.

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:07.740
I like it."

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:08.740
Worst: Berkeley

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:12.280
After ten years stuck with her sister, her
sister's boyfriend, and a rough-around-the-edges

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:17.819
wannabe cowboy, the grown-up Little Rock falls
into a state of young rebellion.

00:01:17.819 --> 00:01:22.130
She's yearning for contact with people her
own age, preferably of the attractive male

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:23.610
variety.

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:27.590
She shows just how serious she is when she
takes off with the guitar-playing, weed-smoking

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:28.590
Berkeley.

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:31.829
"Do I look like the person who would have
weed?"

00:01:31.829 --> 00:01:36.279
Although Tallahassee's reaction to the news
is a bit much, we've got to agree with him

00:01:36.279 --> 00:01:38.679
that the guy's pretty insufferable.

00:01:38.679 --> 00:01:43.549
Not only does he shrug off Little Rock's interests,
he also flat-out lies to her, claiming to

00:01:43.549 --> 00:01:47.909
have composed Bob Dylan's "Like a Rolling
Stone" and Lynyrd Skynyrd's "Free Bird."

00:01:47.909 --> 00:01:52.319
Plus, while his commitment to pacifism is
arguably admirable, roping Little Rock into

00:01:52.319 --> 00:01:56.999
a more defenseless lifestyle seems reckless
at best, and deadly at worst.

00:01:56.999 --> 00:02:00.520
"I have nothing against hippies, I just want
to beat the s--- out of them."

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:01.520
Best: Rules vs. Commandments

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:06.409
The extended, unexplained doppelgänger sequence
in Zombieland: Double Tap is a bit of a mixed

00:02:06.409 --> 00:02:11.091
bag - and not just because they cast Thomas
Middleditch as the double for Jesse Eisenberg

00:02:11.091 --> 00:02:13.310
in a world where Michael Cera exists.

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:15.129
"They said, 'Didn't you know?

00:02:15.129 --> 00:02:16.819
You're a poor man's Jesse Eisenberg.'

00:02:16.819 --> 00:02:17.819
I said, 'what?'"

00:02:17.819 --> 00:02:21.719
But it's all worth it for the scene in which
Columbus and Flagstaff realize that they each

00:02:21.719 --> 00:02:27.560
keep their own list of rules, or in Flagstaff's
case, "commandments," for surviving in a zombie-filled

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:28.739
world.

00:02:28.739 --> 00:02:32.989
With manic politeness, the two nerds compare
notes, kicking themselves when the other has

00:02:32.989 --> 00:02:37.950
a rule they should've included, such as Flagstaff's
first commandment, "Teamwork."

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:42.950
Columbus' first rule, "cardio," is in fact
on Flagstaff's list as well, but not until

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:44.370
the mid-20s.

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:48.260
While it has little to do with the plot of
the movie, Eisenberg and Middleditch each

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:52.810
bring an earnest eagerness to the scene that
makes the rapid-fire recitation of rules,

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:56.290
and the exceedingly cordial one-upmanship,
a joy to watch.

00:02:56.290 --> 00:02:57.290
"He's so cool."

00:02:57.290 --> 00:02:58.520
Worst: Tallahassee's Blackfoot heritage

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:03.420
Early in the movie, Tallahassee decides to
reveal a secret he's never shared before:

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:07.280
somewhere in his family line, he has ties
to the Blackfoot tribe.

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:11.360
There's no real reason that Tallahassee brings
this info up, and it only ever serves as kind

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:13.459
of a one-note punchline.

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:18.170
While Tallahassee functions as a constant
fountain of crude one-liners, leaning on his

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:22.890
supposed ethnic background as joke fodder
feels cheap and exploitative in a way his

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:24.599
other zingers don't.

00:03:24.599 --> 00:03:28.530
What's more, Double Tap doesn't need those
jokes - they're far from the funniest lines

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:29.530
in the film.

00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:30.530
Best: Tallahassee's big leap

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:34.389
When the heroes find themselves up against
a horde of zombies and have no firearms, they

00:03:34.389 --> 00:03:35.980
have to get creative.

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:40.240
The pacifist Babylon settlement doesn't have
any guns to speak of, but they do have a very

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:43.810
tall tower with a constant party raving on
the rooftop.

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:49.409
In a desperate last stand during the final
battle, the group races up the tower's stairs.

00:03:49.409 --> 00:03:53.769
Tallahassee brings up the rear, slowing the
pursuing zombies in any way he can, including

00:03:53.769 --> 00:03:57.299
rolling barrels down the stairs, Donkey Kong
style.

00:03:57.299 --> 00:04:02.189
At the top, Tallahassee leads the zombies
in a charge, seemingly sacrificing himself

00:04:02.189 --> 00:04:06.029
in order to guide them all over the edge of
the roof to their permanent ends.

00:04:06.029 --> 00:04:11.569
But at the last second, he jumps and grabs
onto a dangling hook, and is ultimately pulled

00:04:11.569 --> 00:04:12.569
to safety.

00:04:12.569 --> 00:04:15.519
It's a thrilling scene, and one of Tallahassee's
most heroic moments.

00:04:15.519 --> 00:04:16.519
Worst: Babylon

00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:21.789
We can see the draw of the pacifist community
of Babylon, and why characters like Berkeley,

00:04:21.789 --> 00:04:25.520
Little Rock, Madison, and even Columbus would
be tempted to stay.

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:29.680
But at the same time, there is a zombie apocalypse
going on.

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:34.340
It's aggravating how much the people of Babylon
seem to completely ignore the reality of the

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:35.340
world they live in.

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:39.060
That they actually melt down weapons they
come across, instead of keeping them locked

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:42.389
up in a safe place, just in case, seems ludicrous.

00:04:42.389 --> 00:04:46.480
When the zombies inevitably arrive, the people
of Babylon still don't seem to realize there's

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:51.030
a threat, making us wonder how on earth this
place has managed to stand for ten minutes,

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:52.069
much less ten years.

00:04:52.069 --> 00:04:53.449
Best: The zombie battles

00:04:53.449 --> 00:04:58.090
As with the first Zombieland, one of the most
enjoyable elements of Double Tap is in its

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:01.419
brutal showdowns between humans and zombies.

00:05:01.419 --> 00:05:05.940
The first smackdown, following the core foursome
as they slash a path to the White House, is

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:09.870
a great way to reintroduce the film's main
characters after ten years away.

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:14.889
Later, when Flagstaff and Albuquerque become
infected in the Hound Dog Hotel, the frantic

00:05:14.889 --> 00:05:18.070
fight through the hallways is a thing of beauty.

00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:22.900
But probably the best battle comes at the
end of the film, culminating in Nevada and

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:27.940
the gang crushing dozens of zombies in Albuquerque's
monster truck, "Big Fat Death."

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:31.960
That Columbus is in the back seat getting
carsick as the scene goes on is just icing

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:32.960
on the cake.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:33.960
Worst: Inconsistent T-800 zombies

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:37.640
The first time we meet one of Double Tap's
evolved super-zombies, Columbus dubs it the

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:40.610
T-800, a reference to the Terminator movies.

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:45.349
There's a good reason why: Tallahassee is
forced to go far beyond the double tap in

00:05:45.349 --> 00:05:50.780
order to put the first T-800 down, unloading
nine bullets into its undead body and then

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:54.919
crushing its skull under his boot before it
finally stops trying to eat him.

00:05:54.919 --> 00:05:56.720
"Oh god, those are T-800s.

00:05:56.720 --> 00:05:59.099
Or at the very least T-700s."

00:05:59.099 --> 00:06:03.650
Later in the film, the T-800s have suddenly
become much easier to kill, with characters

00:06:03.650 --> 00:06:07.480
easily taking out T-800s with a single blow
from a shovel.

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:11.990
If Double Tap was dead set on introducing
a new strain of super-zombies, it would've

00:06:11.990 --> 00:06:15.930
been nice for them to stay consistent, and
maybe effect the story a little more than

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:16.930
they end up doing.

00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:17.930
Best: Bill Murray

00:06:17.930 --> 00:06:22.539
One of the best parts of the original Zombieland
is Ghostbusters actor Bill Murray's extended

00:06:22.539 --> 00:06:27.100
cameo as a version of himself, disguised as
a zombie to survive the apocalypse with the

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:28.630
power of acting.

00:06:28.630 --> 00:06:32.860
Unfortunately, Columbus doesn't realize that
Murray is only pretending, and shoots the

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:34.979
actor dead in his Hollywood mansion.

00:06:34.979 --> 00:06:39.449
In Double Tap, we find out that mistakenly
killing a human that you thought was a zombie

00:06:39.449 --> 00:06:41.590
has come to be known as "Murraying."

00:06:41.590 --> 00:06:48.419
"I'm sorry, he just gets me.

00:06:48.419 --> 00:06:50.199
But it still is sad."

00:06:50.199 --> 00:06:54.370
Since Columbus killed Bill Murray in the first
movie, we hadn't held out a lot of hope of

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:55.699
seeing him again in the sequel.

00:06:55.699 --> 00:07:00.009
But the actor does pop up - twice! - during
the end credits.

00:07:00.009 --> 00:07:05.000
Both scenes take place during the 2009 press
junket for the thankfully fictional Garfield

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:10.020
3, which it turns out coincided with the beginning
of the zombie virus outbreak.

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:15.080
The first person to be affected is Today show
host Al Roker, who begins vomiting and becoming

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:16.449
aggressive.

00:07:16.449 --> 00:07:20.750
When more zombies emerge, Murray takes matters
into his own hands, taking them out one after

00:07:20.750 --> 00:07:23.050
another using nothing but a metal folding
chair.

00:07:23.050 --> 00:07:24.050
Worst: Logic

00:07:24.050 --> 00:07:28.360
We're willing to suspend a lot of disbelief
for the Zombieland movies, but in a film that's

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:32.770
supposed to be set a decade into the apocalypse,
there are a few things that are a little hard

00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:34.160
to swallow.

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:35.199
Like really?

00:07:35.199 --> 00:07:37.879
The electricity's still on?

00:07:37.879 --> 00:07:42.210
In the first film, most of our issues could
be handwaved away because of the time period

00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:46.810
- the zombie apocalypse had only been going
on for a short time, so of course the characters

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:52.599
would have a relatively easy time finding
food, clothing, fuel, clean water, and other

00:07:52.599 --> 00:07:53.599
resources.

00:07:53.599 --> 00:07:56.449
After ten years, though, some things should
be a little harder to come by.

00:07:56.449 --> 00:08:01.409
We're not asking for the gritty grimness of
The Walking Dead - a series which is "totally

00:08:01.409 --> 00:08:05.580
unrealistic," according to Columbus - but
at least a little acknowledgement that the

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:08.979
world has actually collapsed by now would've
been appreciated.

